## Object storage / chunks

### `chunk`
Content-addressed, **per-user-scoped** (migration 011 — encrypted chunks
can only dedupe within one user, since different AMKs produce different
ciphertext for identical plaintext).
| Field | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `user` | record\<user\> | READONLY |
| `hash` | string | SHA-256 hex of encrypted bytes, READONLY |
| `size` | int | READONLY, byte size of encrypted blob |
| `storage_key` | string | READONLY, S3 key: `chunks/{user}/{hash[0:2]}/{hash[2:4]}/{hash}` |
| `ref_count` | int | DEFAULT 0 — array-slot references across `media.chunks` + `media_version.chunks`; 0 → garbage collect row + S3 blob |
| `created_at` | datetime | VALUE `time::now()`, READONLY |

Unique index on `(user, hash)`.

---

## Per-client databases & billing

### `client_database`
Up to 5 per `oauth_client` (enforced in the resolver, not the schema).
| Field | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `client` | record\<oauth_client\> | READONLY |
| `name` | string | READONLY — caller-chosen label |
| `db_name` | string | READONLY — provisioned SurrealDB database name |
| `schema_def` | option\<object\> | custom schema (DDL source of truth); `schema_def.*` is `TYPE object FLEXIBLE` (migration 016) to allow arbitrary nested structure (SurrealDB validates nested SCHEMAFULL fields, so a wildcard+FLEXIBLE field is required) |
| `status` | string | DEFAULT "active", one of `active`/`suspended`/`deleted` |
| `created_at` | datetime | VALUE `time::now()`, READONLY |
| `updated_at` | datetime | VALUE `time::now()` |

Unique index on `(client, name)`.

### Billing tables

All four share the same shape (`entity`, `unit_price`, `currency`,
`status`, `invoice_id`, `ref`, `created_at`, plus a type-specific quantity
field and `occurred_at`/`start_time`):

| Table | Quantity field | `status` values | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `billing_storage_period` | `tier` (`hot`/`cold`/`archive`) + `size_bytes` (decimal), `start_time`/`end_time` (open interval) | `pending`/`invoiced`/`void` | Storage billed as GB-months: `(size_bytes / 1_073_741_824) * (end_time - start_time).seconds / 2_629_800` |
| `billing_compute_event` | `units` (decimal seconds) | same | Compute usage |
| `billing_api_event` | `units` (decimal call count) | same | API usage |
| `billing_provisioning_event` | `resource` (e.g. `"database"`) + `units` | same | One-off provisioning charges (e.g. creating a `client_database`) |

`entity` is `record<user | oauth_client>` — either a user or an OAuth
client can be billed. `ref` is an optional pointer to the record that
caused the event (e.g. a `media_version` or `client_database`).
Helper constructors live in `storage/database/billing.go`
(`CreateProvisioningBillingEvent`, `CreateComputeBillingEvent`,
`CreateAPIBillingEvent`).

---

## Rate limiting

### `rate_limit`
Schema defined (migration 007) but **not yet wired into the Go
middleware** — reserved for future use.
| Field | Type | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `bucket` | string | app-built composite key, e.g. `ip:1.2.3.4:POST:/oauth/token` |
| `window` | datetime | floored timestamp for the current window |
| `count` | int | DEFAULT 0 |
| `expires_at` | datetime | `window + window_duration`, for purge |

Unique index on `(bucket, window)`; index on `expires_at` for a background
purge job.

---

## Storage backends (non-SurrealDB)

### Redis (`storage/cache`)
All keys are ephemeral (TTL-based), keeping short-lived OAuth state out of
SurrealDB:
- `code:{code}` — authorization code (TTL = code expiry)
- `rt_lock:{jti}` — refresh-token rotation lock (30s, `SetNX` for replay
  detection)
- `revoked:{jti}` — revoked access-token JTIs (TTL = token expiry)
- `login_challenge:{id}` / `consent_challenge:{id}` — login/consent flow
  state
- `device_invite:{token}` (10 min) / `device_invite_result:{token}` (1h) —
  device-invite/QR approval flow
- `sso:{token}` — SSO session → user ID mapping

### MinIO / S3 (`storage/objectstore`)
- `PutChunk` / `GetChunk` / `DeleteChunk` — encrypted blob storage, keyed by
  `chunks/{userID}/{hash[0:2]}/{hash[2:4]}/{hash}` (sharded for scale).
- `EnsureBucket` — idempotent bucket creation on startup.
